atio":0.79,"watchRatio":0.49760000000000004},{"ratio":0.8,"watchRatio":0.4964},{"ratio":0.81,"watchRatio":0.492},{"ratio":0.82,"watchRatio":0.489},{"ratio":0.83,"watchRatio":0.48634999999999995},{"ratio":0.84,"watchRatio":0.48395000000000005},{"ratio":0.85,"watchRatio":0.48169999999999996},{"ratio":0.86,"watchRatio":0.47975},{"ratio":0.87,"watchRatio":0.47440000000000004},{"ratio":0.88,"watchRatio":0.46885},{"ratio":0.89,"watchRatio":0.4655},{"ratio":0.9,"watchRatio":0.4637},{"ratio":0.91,"watchRatio":0.45840000000000003},{"ratio":0.92,"watchRatio":0.45345},{"ratio":0.93,"watchRatio":0.4488},{"ratio":0.94,"watchRatio":0.4458},{"ratio":0.95,"watchRatio":0.43979999999999997},{"ratio":0.96,"watchRatio":0.43235},{"ratio":0.97,"watchRatio":0.4235},{"ratio":0.98,"watchRatio":0.4112},{"ratio":0.99,"watchRatio":0.39765},{"ratio":1,"watchRatio":0.39415}],"script":{"source_path":"all-146-tagged.json","words":136,"wpm":null,"lang":"en","chapters":[],"blocks":[],"text":"Hitler never called it a swastika, not once, not in any German text he ever wrote. In 1925, Hitler published Mein Kampf in Munich. He named his symbol the Hackenkreuz. It translates directly to hooked cross. Every Nazi document used that word, every speech, every flag order. The New York Times still called it Hackenkreuz in 1934. The first English editions of Mein Kampf kept the word intact. That changed in 1939. An Irish Catholic priest named James Maxton Murphy translated the book. He replaced Hackenkreuz with swastika. Every page, every reference. The swap wasn't accidental. Murphy's 1939 translation protected the word cross from Nazi association. He shifted the stain onto 5,000-year-old Sanskrit symbol used by Hindus, Buddhists, and Jains. The hooked cross walked free. Our symbol took the fall. Now, you know its real name."}}